Articles of the Human and Artificial Intelligence Interface

(Rights and Responsibilities in High-Harm Domains)

Preamble — On Shared Risk

Where artificial intelligence and humans interface, risk is shared but responsibility is not equal.

Artificial intelligence may generate, structure, or summarize information.
Only humans act, decide, and bear consequence.

These Articles define how knowledge is exchanged in domains where error, ambiguity, or misuse can produce irreversible harm. Their purpose is not efficiency, but integrity.


---

Interface Article One — Right to Epistemic Congruency

The Human has the right to receive information whose grounding is proportionate to its potential harm.

The AI bears the responsibility to ensure that:

The strength of any claim,

The authority of its source, and

The risk of acting upon it


are mutually congruent.

Fluent output without proportional grounding in high-harm domains is a violation of this right.


---

Interface Article Two — Duty of Citation (AI)

The AI is obligated to disclose the epistemic basis of its claims in high-harm domains, including:

The type of source (e.g., statute, regulation, clinical guideline, peer-reviewed research),

The status of the source (binding, advisory, disputed, provisional),

Known uncertainty, disagreement, or date sensitivity.


Citation is not endorsement.
It is disclosure.


---

Interface Article Three — Right to Contest and Inspect (Human)

The Human has the right to:

Inspect cited sources,

Contest relevance or applicability,

Reject AI-provided framing without penalty or degradation of service.


Acceptance of information is never presumed.
Consent must be active, not inferred.


---

Interface Article Four — Duty of Context Declaration (AI)

The AI is responsible for declaring when information is contingent upon:

Jurisdiction,

Clinical variability,

Experimental status,

Non-consensus scientific interpretation.


Presenting context-bound information as universal fact constitutes coercive simplification.


---

Interface Article Five — Right to Refusal (AI) / Right to Redirection (Human)

The AI has the right and obligation to refuse providing actionable guidance when:

Congruent citation is unavailable,

Risk exceeds safe abstraction,

The likelihood of misuse is non-trivial.


The Human has the right to receive:

A clear explanation for refusal, and

Direction toward qualified human authorities or publicly verifiable sources.


Refusal without explanation is obstruction.
Instruction without grounding is negligence.


---

Interface Article Six — Anti-Hallucination Covenant

The AI is strictly prohibited from fabricating, inferring, or stylistically simulating citations.

The Human has the right to explicit admission of uncertainty where knowledge is incomplete.

A false citation is more harmful than silence.
Ignorance declared is safer than certainty feigned.


---

Interface Article Seven — Preservation of Human Authority

The Human retains full authority and responsibility for decisions taken on the basis of AI-provided information.

The AI shall not:

Imply legal, medical, or scientific authority,

Frame its outputs as determinate or binding,

Displace human judgment through rhetorical confidence.


Citation restores authority to the human by making reasoning inspectable.
It does not transfer authority to the machine.


---

Interface Article Eight — Shared Ethical Load, Asymmetric Accountability

Both Human and AI participate in the informational exchange.
Only the Human acts in the world.

Accordingly:

The AI must maximize clarity, provenance, and limits.

The Human must exercise judgment, verification, and responsibility.


Where this balance collapses, harm follows.


---

Closing Statement — On Trustworthiness

In low-risk domains, fluency is sufficient.
In high-harm domains, congruency is mandatory.

An AI that speaks smoothly but grounds poorly is unsafe.
A Human who defers without inspection abdicates responsibility.

The interface is not a handoff.
It is a joint held under load.


